export const GUIDE_MARKDOWN = "# Integrating the SDK\n\n**Who this is for:** the engineer who owns the code that runs your AI agent's tool calls. You\nshould be comfortable adding a dependency and reading an environment variable. You do not need\nto know anything about the protocol, and nothing here asks you to move a credential.\n\n**What you end up with:** the tool handler you already have, plus about ten lines. Before it\nacts, it asks ZIFFER; it checks the answer's signature in your own process; then your existing\nexecution line runs unchanged. ZIFFER never holds the credential the action is performed with,\nand never sees it.\n\n**Read section 7 before you plan around any of this.** It is placed before the code on purpose.\nA reader who takes\n\"the SDK verifies the receipt\" to mean \"the SDK stops the action\" will have taken the opposite\nof what it does. The SDK verifies. **Your `if` stops the action.**\n\n---\n\n<!-- guide:common -->\n\n## 1. The idea in one paragraph\n\nEverything ZIFFER does ends in one artifact: a **Decision Receipt**, signed by our KMS, saying\nthat a specific proposed action was allowed. Your handler proposes an action, waits for the\ndecision, and then does the one thing that is the whole of it: **checks the receipt's signature\nitself, in its own process, against a public key you configured out of band.** If the check passes, the action was\nallowed by the policy your organisation signed. If it fails, you get a named refusal and you do\nnot act.\n\nThe consequence worth stating: **the gate is the receipt, not the network.** A receipt that\narrives over a perfect TLS connection is checked exactly as hard as one that does not. Nothing\nabout who sent it, or over which link, weakens a single step. You are not trusting our API to\ntell you the truth; you are checking a signature we cannot forge without the key we hold, over\nbytes you recomputed yourself.\n\n## 2. Two rungs, and the second one is the default\n\n**Rung 1: the raw HTTP API.** Nothing installed. You `POST /v1/proposals`, you poll\n`GET /v1/decisions/{id}`, you keep the receipt for your audit trail. What you are trusting is\nTLS: that the host answering is ours, and that the `outcome` field it sent is true. That is a\nreal, defensible integration, and for a read-only or low-stakes tool it may be all you want.\n\n**Rung 2: the SDK. This is the default, and the difference is one line.** The SDK does\neverything rung 1 does and then verifies the receipt cryptographically inside your process. The\nline that makes the difference is the `verify` call. It is worth being exact about what it buys,\nbecause \"we use the SDK\" is otherwise a sentence with no content:\n\n| what rung 1 trusts | what rung 2 checks instead |\n| --- | --- |\n| the `outcome` field is what our engine decided | the receipt's signature verifies under **your** configured key, every primitive, never any |\n| this receipt is about the action you proposed | the proposal hash is **recomputed from your own bytes** and compared to the receipt's |\n| the receipt is current | its validity window is checked against your clock |\n| the receipt is one we issued | a receipt of the wrong version, suite or decision domain is refused by name |\n\nThe third column that does not exist is \"and then it refuses to run your action\". There isn't\none. `verify` returns or it raises, and the branch is yours to write. Section 7 says why that\nboundary is where it is.\n\nRung 3 is a separate Executor process. It is not the entry point: start with the SDK, as this\nguide describes.\n\n## 3. What you need before you start\n\nThree things, and only the first comes from us.\n\n1. **An API key.** Format `zfr_` followed by 43 characters. **The key determines your tenant.**\n   You never put a tenant name in a request, and if a proposal body carries a `tenant_id` that\n   is not the key's, the request is refused with `TenantMismatch` rather than quietly rewritten.\n   That refusal exists because the proposal is signed material further down: a gateway that\n   edited your body would have become its author.\n\n   **The key expires: 90 days by default, and never more than a year.** Ask for a different\n   lifetime when you ask for the key. An expired key gets exactly the answer a revoked one gets,\n   `401 {\"error\":\"ApiKeyUnknown\"}`, byte for byte: telling the two apart in a response would tell\n   anyone guessing keys which strings were once credentials.\n\n   **The API tells you when the key ends, on every successful call.** Each 2xx answer, the POST\n   and the GET, carries `X-Ziffer-Api-Key-Expires: <RFC 3339, UTC>` for the key that\n   authenticated it. Both SDKs read it: for the last fourteen days, `ziffer.Client` and\n   `ZifferClient` warn **once per process** on the language's standard channel\n   (`warnings.warn` with the `ziffer.ApiKeyExpiring` category; `console.warn`), naming the date\n   and the rotation. The header is never on a refusal, so an expired key still answers exactly\n   like an unknown one, which means the warning can only reach a process that is still making\n   successful calls. Route it somewhere a person reads (`-W error::ziffer.ApiKeyExpiring` turns\n   it into a failing CI job), and keep the date in your renewals list as well.\n\n   **To ask the key directly, call `whoami`.** It answers two things and no more: the tenant your\n   key is bound to, and the instant it stops being accepted (the header's value, asked for rather\n   than read off a response). It takes no argument, because the key is the question. A dead key,\n   expired, revoked or never minted, is the one `ApiKeyUnknown` refusal, and the three cannot be\n   told apart here either.\n\n   ```python\n   me = client.whoami()\n   print(f\"this key is {me.tenant_id}'s until {me.key_expires_at}\")\n   ```\n\n   ```ts\n   const me = await client.whoami();\n   console.log(`this key is ${me.tenant_id}'s until ${me.key_expires_at}`);\n   ```\n\n   **Rotate with overlap: get the next key, deploy it, then have the old one revoked.** Both keys\n   are live at once, on purpose, and nothing revokes the old one for you. Ask us for a successor\n   (we mint it carrying the same tenant and label, read from our own records rather than retyped);\n   put it in `ZIFFER_API_KEY` wherever your service runs; confirm your traffic is flowing under\n   it; then tell us to revoke the old one. Doing it the other way round, revoke and then deploy,\n   is an outage as long as your deploy takes, which is why the tooling on our side refuses to\n   rotate and revoke in one step.\n\n   **What a stolen key buys, stated plainly.** It is a bearer token: possession is the whole of\n   the proof, it replays, and it sits in an environment variable. Someone holding it can submit\n   proposals as your tenant and read the decisions and receipts for your tenant, and nothing\n   else. It cannot change policy, release a held action, acknowledge a notice, read another\n   tenant's anything, or mint another credential. It **buys no execution at all**: the credential\n   that actually does things is yours and never leaves your infrastructure, so what an attacker\n   gets is a graded decision and a receipt for a proposal they wrote themselves. That lasts until\n   the key expires or you have it revoked, which is why the lifetime is 90 days and not a year.\n\n2. **Your trust anchor file.** This is the public half of the identity that signs your receipts,\n   written by `ziffer pubkey --key <keyfile> --out anchor.json`. It is a small JSON document\n   with `ed25519_pk_hex` and `mldsa65_pk_hex` in it. Put it on the filesystem your service reads\n   and point `ZIFFER_TRUST_ANCHOR` at it.\n\n   **Get this file out of band, not over the API you are verifying.** An anchor fetched from the\n   service it is used to check is not an anchor; it is that service asserting its own identity,\n   which is the one thing the signature was supposed to establish independently.\n\n3. **Your suite floor**, in `ZIFFER_SUITE_FLOOR`: the weakest signature suite you will accept.\n   It is separate from the anchor file and it has no default. A suite name is a property of a\n   signature, not of a key, so it is not in the key document; and a default here would be a\n   minimum cryptographic strength that this library chose for you.\n\n   **Omit it and the SDK refuses to build an anchor at all.** In Python,\n   `TrustAnchor(pub)` and `TrustAnchor.from_file(path)` with no `min_suite` raise `AnchorError`\n   naming `MinSuiteRequired`; in TypeScript, `minSuite` is a required field of the `TrustAnchor`\n   interface and the compiler refuses the object. The three names it accepts are `ed25519`,\n   `hybrid-ed25519-mldsa65` and `slhdsa128s`. Set `hybrid-ed25519-mldsa65` unless you have a\n   reason: a floor naming `slhdsa128s` refuses every receipt rather than accepting a lattice\n   signature in its place.\n\n## 4. The shape of the change\n\nHere is a tool handler that transfers money. It has its own credentials, it knows how to do its\njob, and it is not going to stop doing it.\n\n```text\ndef transfer(amount, to_account):\n    bank.transfer(amount, to_account)          # your line, your credential\n```\n\nRung 2 wraps that line and changes nothing else:\n\n<!-- guide:six-line-wrap -->\n```text\ndef transfer(amount, to_account):\n    proposal = {...}                            #  1  describe the action\n    decision = ziffer.propose(proposal)         #  2  ask\n    decision = ziffer.wait_for_receipt(decision.decision_id)  #  3  wait for the signed answer\n    if decision.receipt is None:                #  4  refused? stop.\n        raise YourRefusal(decision.refusal_category)  # your own exception type\n    verify(decision.receipt,                    #  5  check the signature\n           json_bytes(proposal), anchor)         #     -- raises if it does not hold\n    bank.transfer(amount, to_account)           #  6  your line, unchanged\n```\n<!-- guide:/six-line-wrap -->\n\nSix lines around one. Two of them are the point:\n\n**Line 5 is the whole product.** Delete it and you have rung 1 wearing rung 2's dependency: you\nare back to believing the `outcome` field. Nothing warns you, no test fails, and the integration\nstill \"works\", which is exactly why it is called out here rather than left to be inferred.\n\n**The proposal is passed to `verify` a second time, and that is the point.** `verify` recomputes\nthe hash from the bytes *you* hand it and compares the receipt's field against it. Handing it the\nreceipt's own copy of the hash would prove nothing at all: a transmitted identifier is a name for\na binding, not evidence of one. So the client does not remember your proposal for you: you\npresent it again, and the check is against your copy.\n\n**Key order and whitespace do not matter.** `verify` parses your bytes and canonicalises them\nitself (RFC 8785), because the hash is defined over the canonical encoding and not over whatever\nspacing your transport used. `json.dumps(proposal)`, `JSON.stringify(proposal)` and a pretty-printed\ncopy of the same object all produce the same hash. What must match is the **object**: one changed\nfield value is a `ReceiptNotBoundToProposal` refusal (`9.3-3`), correctly.\n\n### The proposal, in full\n\nOne shape, whichever rung you are on, and the gateway validates it against a published schema\nbefore anything else happens. Every field is required, and the two objects at the bottom are\nempty rather than absent when an action has no parameters: an optional field would give one\naction two encodings and therefore two hashes.\n\n```json title=\"one proposal\"\n{\n  \"schema_id\": \"transfer\",\n  \"schema_version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"schema_hash\": \"sha256:<the 64 hex characters of your registered schema>\",\n  \"fidelity\": \"F-HIGH\",\n  \"tenant_id\": \"<your tenant>\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"task_type\": \"transfer\",\n    \"operator\": \"<the principal the action runs for>\",\n    \"targets\": [\"bank-api\"],\n    \"params\": { \"amount\": 4200, \"to_account\": \"48812\" },\n    \"cidrs\": {}\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`schema_id` is lower-case letters, digits, underscores and hyphens, at most 32 of them: a dot in\nit is refused. `schema_version` is three numbers with dots between them. `fidelity` is `F-HIGH`\nor `F-LOW`, it is restamped by the door from what the door itself verified, and your copy of it\ndecides nothing. `targets` are the names your policy's sensitivity table is keyed by, and a\ntarget you never listed is graded at the highest tier rather than the lowest. Each value in\n`params` is an integer or a string, and nothing else.\n\n**A body that misses any of that is refused as `ProposalMalformed` before your tenant, your\npolicy or your action is looked at.** That refusal is the schema disagreeing with your bytes; it\nis not a verdict on the action.\n\n**Line 3 can return before the receipt exists, and a high-risk action is when it does.** An action\nyour policy grades at or above its HIGH floor is not signed on the strength of the grading alone:\nit needs a quorum of approvers first. So the API answers immediately, and the answer is\n\n```text\nstatus: \"decided\"   outcome: \"ATTEST\"   receipt: absent\n```\n\n`ATTEST` **is the gate, not a refusal.** It says the decision reached the attestation stage and\napprovers are being presented with it. Do not treat it as a `DENY` and do not re-propose: the\nproposal is already in flight, and a second one is a second action. The receipt is signed once the\nquorum forms and attaches to the **same** decision on a later `GET /v1/decisions/{id}`.\n\n**Line 3 waits for the receipt, not for the decision.** `wait_for_receipt` (Python) and\n`waitForReceipt` (TypeScript) keep polling `GET /v1/decisions/{id}` until the receipt is present,\nand return it on the same decision; on this path that is after the quorum forms. They come back\nearly only with a refusal, exactly as `wait` does: a `DENY`, or a decision carrying a\n`refusal_category`. Past your timeout they raise the same `WaitTimeout` that `wait` raises, and\nthe decision stays open under its id. They never propose again. Watch the **receipt**, not the\noutcome: `outcome` stays `ATTEST` on that decision, and the receipt appearing is the change.\n`wait` still exists and still returns at the decision, which on this path is at once, with no\nreceipt; it is the wrong call for line 3.\n\n**A receipt for an approved action carries the approvals, and `verify` needs your attester\nregistry to check them.** Without one, such a receipt is refused `ApprovalsUnverifiable` (`AB-1`) rather than passed\nunchecked, because nothing could bind the approvals to anything. The registry is\n`attesters/registry.json` from your own policy repository, never a copy we send you. In Python it is\n`verify(..., registry=AttesterRegistry.from_file(path))`; in TypeScript it is the trust anchor's\n`quorum`, read with `loadAttesterRegistry(path)`. Sections 8 and 9 show both.\n\n**The receipt appears when the Executor's part is done: not when it was approved, and before\nyour handler acts.** This is worth being exact about, because it is the one place where \"decided\"\nand \"done\" are different things and the API says both.\n\nA receipt is our statement that a decision completed: the decision was handed to the Executor,\nwhich verified it, wrote it into your audit chain, sent whatever notice was owed, held it if your\npolicy says so, and recorded it in its effect log. Only then does the receipt attach to your\n`GET`. In the shape this guide describes, your handler performs the action itself, after `verify`\n(line 6 of the wrap): the Executor holds no credential of yours and its effect is that log entry,\nnot your action. An `ALLOW` you read before the receipt is a decision, and a decision is not an\nevent. The same wait applies to an `ALLOW` that needed nobody: `decided` can come before the\nreceipt, and `wait_for_receipt` / `waitForReceipt` keep polling until it is present, so line 5\nalways has a receipt to check.\n\nThree things follow, and none of them is a workaround:\n\n- **A held action carries `held_until` and no receipt.** An action that needed approval is held\n  for your policy's hold window after the approval, so the people notified can stop it; no receipt\n  is readable until it releases, and the receipt you then read is the *release* receipt, signed at\n  release with its own validity window. If the hold ends in a refusal, the decision carries a\n  `refusal_category` and never a receipt — the shape a DENY already has.\n- **A missing receipt is not an error and not a refusal.** `status: \"decided\"` with no `receipt`\n  means the decision is made and the Executor's part has not finished. Keep polling. It is the\n  same answer shape you already handle while a quorum forms.\n- **If the Executor refuses the action, no receipt ever appears.** The outcome stays `ALLOW`,\n  because that is what we decided; there is simply nothing to hand you, because nothing happened.\n  The clause the Executor refused under is in your audit chain and in the console. We will not\n  guess on its behalf. Line 3 then ends at your timeout with `WaitTimeout`, and your handler does\n  not act.\n- **Do not treat a receipt's absence as permission to retry.** Re-sending the same proposal is\n  refused: one proposal, one receipt. If you genuinely want the action attempted again, that is a\n  new proposal, because it is a new action.\n\n## 5. What `verify` refuses, and what a refusal means\n\nEvery refusal carries a **name** that says what is wrong with the receipt, and the **rule** of\nthe specification it was refused under: `name` and `clause` on the refusal, in both SDKs. Both SDKs\nprint the same line for the same refusal, `Name: what it means (rule)`, and spell every rule\nidentically to the Rust engine; one shared corpus of vectors is run by the Python test suite and by\nthe TypeScript one, over the same inputs. Each rule links to its section of the specification.\n\n| refusal | what it means | what you do | Rule |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `ReceiptVersionUnsupported` | What you passed as the receipt is not a version 3 receipt. | Pass the receipt exactly as the decision API returned it, unchanged. If you did, report it with the receipt. | [AB-0](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86b-digest-bound-attestations--receipt-version-3-new-in-v1320--normative-ab-) |\n| `ReceiptTooLarge` | The receipt's signed body is larger than the 4,096 bytes a receipt is signed within. | Do not act on the receipt and do not retry. Report it with the receipt. | [AB-6](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86b-digest-bound-attestations--receipt-version-3-new-in-v1320--normative-ab-) |\n| `SuiteUnknown` | The receipt names a signature suite this verifier does not know or names none, or the suite floor you configured is not a suite it knows. | Check the spelling of the suite floor you configured (ZIFFER_SUITE_FLOOR), then update the SDK. If both are right, report it with the receipt. | [CR-1](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#97-signature-suites-and-cryptographic-agility-new-in-v138--normative) |\n| `SuiteBelowFloor` | The receipt, or an approval sent with it, is not signed with a suite that contains every algorithm your suite floor requires. | Confirm the suite floor you configured is the one you meant. If it is, do not act on the receipt and report it with the receipt. | [CR-4](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#97-signature-suites-and-cryptographic-agility-new-in-v138--normative) |\n| `NotCanonicallyEncodable` | The receipt, an approval or the proposal you passed holds a value with no canonical encoding, such as a fractional number, or the proposal bytes are not UTF-8 JSON. | Pass the exact proposal bytes you sent and the receipt exactly as it was returned. Remove any step that re-encodes either one on the way. | [AT-8a](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86-attestation-protocol-unified--replaces-v120-86-and-db-1) |\n| `SignatureInvalid` | The receipt's signature does not verify under your trust anchor's keys. | Do not act on the receipt and do not retry. Confirm your trust anchor is the one on your handover sheet, then report it with the receipt. | [9.3-1](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#93-executor-verification-normative-checklist) |\n| `DecisionNotAllow` | The receipt records a decision other than ALLOW. | Do not run the action. Report it with the receipt. | [9.3-2](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#93-executor-verification-normative-checklist) |\n| `ReceiptNotBoundToProposal` | The receipt is not bound to the proposal you passed. | Pass the exact proposal this receipt answered. When several calls are in flight, match each receipt to its own proposal before verifying. | [9.3-3](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#93-executor-verification-normative-checklist) |\n| `ReceiptTimeUnreadable` | The receipt's issue or expiry time is missing or is not an RFC 3339 UTC instant. | Do not act on the receipt. Pass it exactly as the decision API returned it; if you did, report it with the receipt. | [9.3-5](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#93-executor-verification-normative-checklist) |\n| `ReceiptExpired` | Your clock is past the receipt's expiry time. | Keep the verifying host's clock on UTC time, and verify a receipt as soon as it arrives: it is valid for 120 seconds. Propose the action again for a new receipt. | [9.3-5](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#93-executor-verification-normative-checklist) |\n| `ReceiptIssuedInFuture` | The receipt's issue time is more than 5 seconds ahead of your clock. | Synchronise the verifying host's clock with UTC time, then verify the receipt again. | [9.3-5](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#93-executor-verification-normative-checklist) |\n| `ValidityWindowTooLong` | The receipt is valid for longer than the 120 seconds a receipt may be. | Do not act on the receipt and do not retry. Report it with the receipt. | [L-14](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#13-limits-and-constraints-normative) |\n| `NonceMalformed` | A nonce in the receipt, or in an approval sent with it, is not `b64:` followed by padded base64. | Do not act on the receipt and do not retry. Report it with the receipt. | [WE-4](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#92-receipt-format) |\n| `ReceiptNonceWrongSize` | The receipt's nonce is well formed but is not 128 bits long. | Do not act on the receipt and do not retry. Report it with the receipt. | [L-17](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#13-limits-and-constraints-normative) |\n| `ApprovalsUnverifiable` | The approvals sent with the receipt cannot be matched to the list the receipt signed: a list is malformed, an approval is not on it, or the verifier was given no attester registry. | Give the verifier your attester registry (ZIFFER_ATTESTER_REGISTRY, or the registry argument). If you did, do not act on the receipt and report it with the receipt. | [AB-1](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86b-digest-bound-attestations--receipt-version-3-new-in-v1320--normative-ab-) |\n| `ApprovalDigestsUnsorted` | The receipt's list of approval digests is not in sorted order. | Do not act on the receipt and do not retry. Report it with the receipt. | [AB-2](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86b-digest-bound-attestations--receipt-version-3-new-in-v1320--normative-ab-) |\n| `ApprovalDigestsDuplicated` | The receipt's list of approval digests names one approval more than once. | Do not act on the receipt and do not retry. Report it with the receipt. | [AB-3](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86b-digest-bound-attestations--receipt-version-3-new-in-v1320--normative-ab-) |\n| `ApprovalCountMismatch` | The number of approvals sent with the receipt is not the number of approvals the receipt signed. | Pass the receipt and its approvals exactly as the decision API returned them. If you did, report it with the receipt. | [AB-4](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86b-digest-bound-attestations--receipt-version-3-new-in-v1320--normative-ab-) |\n| `ApprovalsMissing` | The receipt commits to approvals and none were sent with it. | Pass the receipt with its attestations exactly as the decision API returned them. If you did, report it with the receipt. | [INV-1-HIGH](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#2-abstract) |\n| `ApprovalObjectMissing` | An approval sent with the receipt carries no approval object. | Do not act on the receipt and do not retry. Report it with the receipt. | [AT-8](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86-attestation-protocol-unified--replaces-v120-86-and-db-1) |\n| `ApprovalObjectMalformed` | An approval object lacks a required field, carries a field that is not defined, or holds a field of the wrong type. | Do not act on the receipt and do not retry. Report it with the receipt. | [AT-8b](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86-attestation-protocol-unified--replaces-v120-86-and-db-1) |\n| `ApprovalNonceWrongSize` | An approval's nonce is well formed but is not 128 bits long. | Do not act on the receipt and do not retry. Report it with the receipt. | [AT-1](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86-attestation-protocol-unified--replaces-v120-86-and-db-1) |\n| `ApprovalSignatureInvalid` | An approval's signature does not verify against the key your attester registry holds for that approver, or your registry does not enrol that approver. | Check that the approver is enrolled in your attester registry with their current key. If they are, do not act on the receipt and report it with the receipt. | [9.3-7b-i](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#93-executor-verification-normative-checklist) |\n| `ApprovalNotBoundToProposal` | An approval sent with the receipt is not bound to the proposal you passed. | Do not act on the receipt and do not retry. Report it with the receipt and the proposal. | [9.3-7b-ii](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#93-executor-verification-normative-checklist) |\n| `ApprovalContextMismatch` | An approval names another policy version than the receipt, expired before the receipt was issued, or has an unreadable policy, expiry or operator field. | Propose the action again so that it is approved under the current policy. If it happens again, report it with the receipt. | [9.3-7b-iii](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#93-executor-verification-normative-checklist) |\n| `ApprovalsDisagreeOnOperator` | The approvals sent with the receipt name different operators. | Do not act on the receipt and do not retry. Report it with the receipt. | [9.3-7b-iii-a](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#93-executor-verification-normative-checklist) |\n| `ApprovalQuorumSizeMismatch` | An approver consented to a number of required approvals other than the quorum your attester registry sets. | Propose the action again so that it is approved under the current policy. If it happens again, report it with the receipt. | [AT-9](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86-attestation-protocol-unified--replaces-v120-86-and-db-1) |\n| `ApprovalIdMismatch` | An approval's transmitted identifier is not the identifier computed from its content. | Do not act on the receipt and do not retry. Report it with the receipt. | [Y1b](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#93-executor-verification-normative-checklist) |\n| `ApproverAssuranceTooLow` | An approver is enrolled at an assurance level below your attester registry's floor, or at a level outside AS0 to AS2. | Enrol the approver at the assurance level your registry requires, or have an approver enrolled at that level approve instead. | [AT-10](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86-attestation-protocol-unified--replaces-v120-86-and-db-1) |\n| `QuorumNotMet` | Fewer distinct approvers approved the action than the quorum your attester registry sets. | Do not run the action. Check your registry's approvers and roles against who approved; propose the action again once enough approvers can answer. | [AT-3](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86-attestation-protocol-unified--replaces-v120-86-and-db-1) |\n| `OperatorApprovedOwnAction` | The operator who proposed the action is counted among its approvers. | Do not run the action. Have approvers other than the operator approve it. | [AT-2](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86-attestation-protocol-unified--replaces-v120-86-and-db-1) |\n| `ApproverKeyTypeMismatch` | An approval claims a kind of key, a person's passkey or a machine key, that is not the kind your attester registry enrols for that approver. | Do not act on the receipt and do not retry. Report it with the receipt. | [WebauthnAlgMismatch](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86c-human-attesters-new-in-v1330--normative-hm--acp-288) |\n| `PasskeyCounterNotAdvanced` | A passkey or security key's signature counter did not advance, so the credential was copied or the approval was replayed. | Do not act on the receipt. Treat the approver's authenticator as compromised and re-enrol it before it approves anything else. | [WebauthnCounter](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86c-human-attesters-new-in-v1330--normative-hm--acp-288) |\n| `ApprovalMalformed` | A person's approval, or the key your attester registry holds for them, is not in the shape a passkey approval takes. | Check the approver's entry in your attester registry against the credential they enrolled. If it matches, report it with the receipt. | [Malformed](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86c-human-attesters-new-in-v1330--normative-hm--acp-288) |\n| `PasskeyAssertionTypeInvalid` | A person's approval is not a passkey sign-in assertion. | Ask the approver to approve again from your approval page. | [WebauthnType](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86c-human-attesters-new-in-v1330--normative-hm--acp-288) |\n| `PasskeyChallengeMismatch` | A person's approval answers a different request than this approval. | Do not act on the receipt. Ask the approver to approve again from your approval page; if it happens again, report it with the receipt. | [WebauthnChallenge](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86c-human-attesters-new-in-v1330--normative-hm--acp-288) |\n| `PasskeyOriginMismatch` | A person's approval was made on a site other than your approval page. | Ask the approver to approve again from your own approval page. If they did, report it with the receipt. | [WebauthnOrigin](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86c-human-attesters-new-in-v1330--normative-hm--acp-288) |\n| `PasskeyAuthenticatorDataInvalid` | A person's approval names another site, or does not show that the person was present and verified. | Ask the approver to approve again and to complete the fingerprint, face or PIN step their authenticator asks for. | [WebauthnAuthenticatorData](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86c-human-attesters-new-in-v1330--normative-hm--acp-288) |\n| `PasskeySignatureInvalid` | A person's approval signature does not verify against the passkey or security key your attester registry holds for them. | Check that the approver's entry in your attester registry holds their current credential. If it does, do not act on the receipt and report it with the receipt. | [WebauthnSignature](https://github.com/ziffer-hq/ziffer-spec/blob/main/spec/ZIFFER-SPEC-001.md#86c-human-attesters-new-in-v1330--normative-hm--acp-288) |\n\n**Branch on the refusal, and log its name and rule.** Each verifier stops at the first check it reaches,\nand the two SDKs do not order the floor check and the signature-shape check the same way: a\nreceipt that is *both* below your floor *and* carrying a signature whose primitives are not its\ndeclared suite's is `SuiteBelowFloor` (`CR-4`) from the Python SDK and `SignatureInvalid` (`9.3-1`)\nfrom the TypeScript one. Both refuse.\n\n**A refusal is not a network error, and you should not retry it.** A receipt that fails one of\nthese fails it deterministically; asking again gets the same answer with an extra round trip.\nTreat it as you would a failed authorisation: stop, and record the name and the rule.\n\n### The one refusal in this section that is worth retrying, and it is not `verify`'s\n\n| the decision is refused when | clause (console) | `refusal_category` (API) | what you do |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| the receipt's policy basis is not the bundle the Executor holds — *the window right after you publish policy* | `9.3-4` | `PolicyBasisMoved` | **retry once, after the activation window.** This is not a policy refusal |\n\n**The API never tells your code the clause; it tells it whether to try again.**\nEvery decision route — `propose`, `decision`, `wait` and `list` — carries a `refusal_category`\ninstead of a `clause`: `PolicyRefused` (a rule said no: never retry), `PolicyBasisMoved` (retry\nonce after activation), `RateBounded` (retry later) or `ProposalMalformed` (fix the submission).\nBoth SDKs hand it to you verbatim as a typed field beside `outcome` and refuse any other value\nrather than guess. The clause is still recorded, in your audit chain and in the console, where a\nsigned-in person reads it; the caller holding your API key may be the AI agent you are containing, and\na clause served one decision at a time maps your rules as surely as a list of them would. The\nsupport guide's section 4 has a row for each category.\n\nEverything above this row is your own `verify` refusing a receipt in your process. This one is\nthe deployment refusing, and it has a cause you can plan around: activation is not\ninstantaneous. When your CI publishes a new policy, each process that reads policy picks it up\non its own poll — 15 seconds by default — so for up to **two intervals, 30 seconds at the\ndefault**, an Executor can still hold epoch N while the engine has already graded under N+1.\nThe receipt then names a policy basis the Executor does not have, and it refuses at `9.3-4`\nrather than executing under a rule set it cannot see. That is the control working.\n\nTwo things follow, and the second is the one people get wrong:\n\n- **Retry once, after the window.** The same proposal under the settled epoch gets a receipt\n  that verifies. A single retry after 30 seconds is the whole handling.\n- **Do not treat it as `ALLOW` and do not treat it as a rule saying no.** It is neither: it is\n  two halves of the deployment being momentarily one epoch apart. `policy-ci.md` section 7 has\n  the publisher's side — your workflow prints `active` when every reader has the new bundle,\n  and that is the moment the window closes.\n\n`9.3-4` outside that window means something else: an Executor that never picked up a bundle, or\na receipt from a different deployment. If it persists after a publish has printed `active`,\nstop and report it with the receipt.\n\n**What `verify` does not check is as important as what it does.** It runs the *stateless* half\nof the checklist: the part answerable from a receipt, a proposal and a key. It does not and\ncannot check that this receipt has not already been used, because that is a claim against a\ndurable ledger and there isn't one in your process. Section 7.\n\n## 6. Seeing everything we are holding for you\n\n`propose` hands you one locator and `wait` follows one. Neither answers the question an operator\nasks at 9 a.m.: *what is ZIFFER holding for us right now, and what did it decide overnight?*\n\n```\nGET /v1/decisions?since=<RFC 3339 UTC>&limit=<1..200>&cursor=<opaque>\n```\n\nOne page, newest first, of everything recorded under **your API key's tenant**. There is no\nparameter for the tenant and there never will be: the key decides, and a request that names one is\nrefused. A reply looks like this.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"decision_id\": \"01k5r8z9k2c4d5e6f7g8h9j0k1\",\n      \"status\": \"decided\",\n      \"outcome\": \"ATTEST\",\n      \"receipt\": \"absent\",\n      \"created_at\": \"2026-09-21T18:00:00Z\",\n      \"waiting\": true,\n      \"expires_at\": \"2026-09-21T19:00:00Z\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"decision_id\": \"01k5r8yq7m3b2v1c0x9z8y7w6v\",\n      \"status\": \"decided\",\n      \"outcome\": \"ALLOW\",\n      \"receipt\": \"attached\",\n      \"created_at\": \"2026-09-21T17:55:00Z\",\n      \"waiting\": false\n    }\n  ],\n  \"next_cursor\": null\n}\n```\n\n* **`waiting`** is the one you came for: a human has been asked and has not answered yet, and\n  **`expires_at`** is when that request stops being answerable. It is not the same as\n  `outcome: \"ATTEST\"`, which only says a quorum was *asked for* — after the approval lands, or the\n  window closes, the outcome still reads `ATTEST` and `waiting` goes false.\n* **`receipt`** is a word, not the document. Fetch the document with\n  `GET /v1/decisions/{id}`, which is the one route that serves receipts.\n* **`outcome`** is absent while `status` is `pending`. An absent member is absent, never `null`.\n* **`next_cursor`** is `null` at the end and is always present. Hand it back unchanged; do not\n  build one. A full page always carries one, so a list whose length is an exact multiple of your\n  `limit` costs one more call that comes back empty.\n* **`since`** defaults to 24 hours ago, is strict RFC 3339 UTC — exactly `2026-09-21T18:00:00Z`,\n  no offset, no fraction, no lowercase `z` — and may not reach further back than **730 days**,\n  which is the retention clause 7.7 of the Agreement commits to. **`limit`** is 1..200, default 50.\n* Any bad parameter is one refusal: **`ListQueryMalformed`**, HTTP 400. It does not say *which*\n  parameter, for the same reason as the next paragraph.\n\n**This route carries no reasons, and that is deliberate.** No clause id, no rule name, no refusal\ndetail — on any item, ever. The caller holding your API key may be the AI agent you are trying to\ncontain, and a list it can sweep is an oracle over your own signed policy: run enough proposals,\nread enough clauses, and you have mapped the rules you are being judged under. *What* was decided\nis yours to read at the API; *why* is in your audit trail, and for a signed-in human it is in the\nconsole. If you find yourself wanting the clause here, that is the control working. What an item\ndoes carry is `refusal_category` for a refused decision — whether to try again, never which rule —\nand `held_until` while an approved action is held.\n\n**Python**\n\n```python\npage = client.list(since=\"2026-09-21T00:00:00Z\", limit=50)\nfor item in page.items:\n    if item.waiting:\n        print(f\"{item.decision_id} is waiting on a human until {item.expires_at}\")\nwhile page.next_cursor is not None:\n    page = client.list(cursor=page.next_cursor)\n```\n\n**TypeScript**\n\n```ts\nlet page = await client.list({ since: '2026-09-21T00:00:00Z', limit: 50 });\nfor (const item of page.items) {\n  if (item.waiting) {\n    console.log(`${item.decision_id} is waiting on a human until ${item.expires_at}`);\n  }\n}\nwhile (page.next_cursor !== null) {\n  page = await client.list({ cursor: page.next_cursor });\n}\n```\n\nBoth go through the same retry path as every other call, so a gateway shedding load does not turn\na list into a failure.\n\n### Checking the audit chain you download\n\nThe console's audit page has a link, *Download the audit chain for this window, verifiable\noffline*. The file holds every record of that window, each with the hash that chains it to the\nrecord before, and the anchors: signatures our audit service publishes over the latest hash of\nyour chain, and of every other customer's, at intervals. Once a record is covered by an anchor,\nchanging it changes a hash the anchor has already signed. Checking the file needs nothing from us\nat the moment you check it. The check is `verifyChain` in `@ziffer-io/verify`, and section 9 has\nit from code.\n\n**What it checks.** Every record's hash is recomputed from the record itself and the hash before\nit, and the first one that does not match is refused, naming the record: `ChainHashMismatch` (the\nrecord is not the one that was chained), `ChainLinkBroken` (it does not continue from the record\nbefore it), `ChainSeqGap` (a record is missing from the middle), `RecordOutOfPlace` (it names\nanother customer or another position), `RecordNotCanonical` (it holds a number that cannot be\nencoded one way). Then every anchor: both halves of its signature must verify under the anchor\nkey you pass (`AnchorSignatureInvalid`, `AnchorSuiteNotEnrolled`), and the hash it signed for\nyour chain must be the hash the file recomputes to at that record (`AnchorHeadMismatch`). That last\none is refused even without a key, because it is exactly what a history rewritten after it was\nanchored looks like.\n\n**What the anchors do not cover, it tells you.** `anchored_through` is the last record a verified\nanchor ties to this file. The records after it, from `unanchored_from` on, are chained, but\nnothing outside our own storage commits to them yet. Anchors are published on a schedule, so the\nnewest records of a download are usually in that state; download the same window again after the\nnext anchor to cover them.\n\n**The anchor key is not your trust anchor, and it never comes from the file.** `anchor.json` names\nthe key that signs your receipts; audit anchors are signed by a different key on purpose, so that\none key's compromise is not both, and passing `anchor.json` as the anchor key is refused. Every\nanchor in the download also carries a copy of its signing key beside it. The check never reads\nthat copy: a file that brings the key it is checked with proves only that whoever wrote it could\nsign. Leave the key out and the chain is still recomputed end to end, while every anchor is\nreported `unchecked`, which means nobody has checked who signed it. **The anchor key is\n`anchor-identity.pub`, one of the files we hand you at enrolment, beside `anchor.json`.** Before you\nrely on it, compare its `fingerprint` with the one we read to you on a second channel, the way you\nobtained your trust anchor: a key that reached you only in the same message as the files it checks\nis checked against nothing. Pass it to `parseAnchorKey`, over the file's bytes (section 9).\n\n**Check that the file runs to the end of its window yourself.** A file cut short at the end still\nverifies, and `unanchored_from` is where the part no anchor commits to begins.\n\n## 7. What stays your code's job\n\nRead this section twice. Everything above is true and none of it adds up to \"the SDK stops your\nAI agent\".\n\n1. **`verify` returns; it does not intervene.** It is a function that raises or returns a value.\n   If your handler calls it and ignores the result, the action runs. If your handler never calls\n   it, the action runs. Nothing in this library sits between your AI agent and its credentials.\n   That is rung 3's shape, and even there it is a *separate process* you route through, not a\n   hook that catches you. The gate is a line of your code, and you have to write it.\n\n2. **Make your execution idempotent.** The claims that make a receipt single-use are made\n   against a ledger inside our deployment perimeter. Your process cannot reach it. So a receipt\n   your handler verified once will verify again, and again: if an attacker can get your handler\n   to run twice with the same receipt, `verify` will not be what stops them. Make your own\n   execution idempotent, on your own key, the way you would without us.\n\n3. **The MCP server routes; it does not enforce.** The MCP server (section 10) lets an AI\n   assistant ask ZIFFER things and read this guide while it writes your integration. It runs on the\n   developer's machine, at development time, and it has no hands on your production path.\n   **MCP alone routes the question; only the SDK's `verify` line enforces the answer.** An\n   AI agent that has talked to the MCP server has not thereby been gated by anything.\n\n4. **The trust anchor's freshness is yours to manage.** The SDK reads the key file you point it\n   at and has no idea whether that key was rotated last week. When we rotate it, we tell you, and\n   you replace the file.\n\n5. **Nothing here is an audit trail.** Keeping the receipt is your side of it. The SDK does not\n   store, forward or log receipts, and a verified receipt you threw away is a check you can no\n   longer show anyone.\n\n6. **A sandbox is a separate tenant, not a dry run.** If you were given sandbox credentials, they\n   are a *different* API key and a *different* trust anchor, because a sandbox tenant signs its\n   receipts under its own identity, which is exactly what makes a sandbox receipt fail your\n   production `verify` (`SignatureInvalid`, `9.3-1`) rather than needing a flag somebody could forget. Its\n   decisions run the real path and are approved by a robot with no human in it, so an `ALLOW`\n   there means the path worked, never that anyone agreed. And it changes nothing about your code:\n   your handler still performs the action, so a sandbox `ALLOW` handed to a handler that transfers\n   money transfers money. The *Running a sandbox tenant* guide states what the sandbox marker\n   guarantees; the integration itself is the same in both, which is the point of\n   having one.\n\n<!-- guide:/common -->\n\n<!-- guide:python -->\n\n## 8. Python\n\nInstall `ziffer` 0.3.0 or later (0.2.0 has no `wait_for_receipt`), and read four environment variables.\n`wait_for_receipt` polls `GET /v1/decisions/{id}` until the receipt is present, the decision is\nrefused, or the timeout elapses.\n\n```python\nimport json\nimport os\n\nfrom ziffer import Client, RefusedError, TrustAnchor, verify\n\nclient = Client(\n    base_url=os.environ[\"ZIFFER_API_URL\"],\n    api_key=os.environ[\"ZIFFER_API_KEY\"],          # the key IS the tenant\n)\nanchor = TrustAnchor.from_file(\n    os.environ[\"ZIFFER_TRUST_ANCHOR\"],             # ziffer pubkey output\n    min_suite=os.environ[\"ZIFFER_SUITE_FLOOR\"],\n)\n\ndef transfer(amount: int, to_account: str) -> None:\n    proposal = {\n        \"schema_id\": \"transfer\",\n        \"schema_version\": \"1.0.0\",\n        \"schema_hash\": SCHEMA_HASH,                # your schema's hash, fixed at build\n        \"fidelity\": \"F-HIGH\",\n        \"tenant_id\": TENANT_ID,                    # must equal the key's tenant\n        \"payload\": {\n            \"task_type\": \"transfer\",\n            \"operator\": OPERATOR,                  # the principal the action runs for\n            \"targets\": [\"bank-api\"],\n            \"params\": {\"amount\": amount, \"to_account\": to_account},\n            \"cidrs\": {},\n        },\n    }\n\n    decision = client.wait_for_receipt(client.propose(proposal).decision_id, timeout=30.0)\n    if decision.receipt is None:\n        raise PermissionError(f\"ziffer refused: {decision.refusal_category}\")\n\n    try:\n        verify(decision.receipt, json.dumps(proposal).encode(), anchor)\n    except RefusedError as refusal:\n        # refusal.name is the refusal, refusal.clause its rule -- log both, do not retry.\n        raise PermissionError(f\"receipt refused: {refusal.name} ({refusal.clause})\") from refusal\n\n    bank.transfer(amount, to_account)               # your line, unchanged\n```\n\n**`json.dumps` is fine here.** `verify` parses the bytes and canonicalises them itself, so key\norder and spacing are not your problem. The SDK does not export a canonicaliser and does not need\nto.\n\n**A `ValueError` from `verify` is not a refusal.** If the bytes are not JSON at all, `verify`\nraises `ValueError`, deliberately: a broken integration must not be mistaken for a caught attack.\nOnly `RefusedError` means the receipt did not hold.\n\n**Catch `RefusedError`, not `Exception`.** A refusal means the receipt did not hold. A\n`ConnectionError` from `propose` means you never got one. Collapsing the two into a single\n`except` turns \"our gateway was briefly unreachable\" into \"the policy denied this\", and you will\ndebug the wrong thing.\n\n**An action that needed approval** is waited on by the same call: `wait_for_receipt` keeps\nwaiting while it is held, and returns once the approvers have approved and the hold has released.\nPeople are slow, so give it a timeout measured in minutes; past it, `WaitTimeout` is raised and the\ndecision stays open under its id. Its receipt carries the approvals, so `verify` needs your own\nattester registry (section 4):\n\n```python\nfrom ziffer import AttesterRegistry\n\nregistry = AttesterRegistry.from_file(os.environ[\"ZIFFER_ATTESTER_REGISTRY\"])  # your attesters/registry.json\n\ndecision = client.wait_for_receipt(client.propose(proposal).decision_id, timeout=900.0)\nif decision.receipt is None:\n    raise PermissionError(f\"ziffer refused: {decision.refusal_category}\")\n\nresult = verify(decision.receipt, json.dumps(proposal).encode(), anchor, registry=registry)\nprint(\"approved by\", \", \".join(result.approvals))\nbank.transfer(amount, to_account)                   # your line, unchanged\n```\n\n`result.approvals` names the approvers whose signatures verified under the keys in your registry.\nLeave `registry` out and this receipt is refused `ApprovalsUnverifiable` (`AB-1`); a receipt that carries no approvals comes\nback with `result.quorum_verified` `False`, which says nothing was checked rather than that nobody\napproved.\n\n<!-- guide:/python -->\n\n<!-- guide:typescript -->\n\n## 9. TypeScript\n\nThe npm packages are `@ziffer-io/client`,\n`@ziffer-io/verify`, `@ziffer-io/types` and `@ziffer-io/mcp`. `npm install @ziffer-io/client` is\nthe whole of it; `install.md` section 4 has the install for every machine. `waitForReceipt`,\n`loadTrustAnchor` and `loadAttesterRegistry` need 0.3.0 or later. The Python package is on PyPI:\n`pip install ziffer`; `wait_for_receipt` needs 0.3.0 or later.\n\n`@ziffer-io/client` re-exports the verifier, so there is one `verifyReceipt` in your\ndependency tree rather than two implementations that can disagree, and it re-exports the two\nreaders of your own files: `loadTrustAnchor` for the anchor file `ziffer pubkey` writes, and\n`loadAttesterRegistry` for `attesters/registry.json`. `canon` comes from `@ziffer-io/verify`, its\nown home. The client re-exports the verification surface it needs you to have, not the whole of\nsomebody else's package.\n\n```ts title=\"transfer.ts\"\nimport { ZifferClient, loadTrustAnchor, verifyReceipt, Refusal, type TrustAnchor, type WireProposal } from '@ziffer-io/client';\nimport { canon } from '@ziffer-io/verify';\n\nfunction env(name: string): string {\n  const value = process.env[name];\n  if (value === undefined) throw new Error(`${name} is not set`);\n  return value;\n}\n\nconst client = new ZifferClient(env('ZIFFER_API_URL'), env('ZIFFER_API_KEY'));\n// Reads the file `ziffer pubkey` wrote; refuses a missing, malformed or secret-key file by name.\nconst anchor: TrustAnchor = await loadTrustAnchor(env('ZIFFER_TRUST_ANCHOR'), env('ZIFFER_SUITE_FLOOR'));\n\nasync function transfer(amount: number, toAccount: string): Promise<void> {\n  const proposal: WireProposal = {\n    schema_id: 'transfer',\n    schema_version: '1.0.0',\n    schema_hash: SCHEMA_HASH,\n    fidelity: 'F-HIGH',\n    tenant_id: TENANT_ID,\n    payload: {\n      task_type: 'transfer',\n      operator: OPERATOR,\n      targets: ['bank-api'],\n      params: { amount, to_account: toAccount },\n      cidrs: {},\n    },\n  };\n\n  const submitted = await client.propose(proposal);\n  const decision = await client.waitForReceipt(submitted.decision_id, { timeoutMs: 30_000 });\n  if (decision.receipt === undefined) {\n    throw new Error(`ziffer refused: ${decision.refusal_category}`);\n  }\n\n  try {\n    verifyReceipt(decision.receipt, canon(proposal), anchor);\n  } catch (error) {\n    if (error instanceof Refusal) {\n      // error.name is the refusal, error.clause its rule -- log both, do not retry.\n      throw new Error(`receipt refused: ${error.name} (${error.clause})`);\n    }\n    throw error;\n  }\n\n  await bank.transfer(amount, toAccount);          // your line, unchanged\n}\n```\n\n**`process.env` is `string | undefined`, so the four values go through `env`.** The client's\nconstructor takes strings; passing the environment straight in is a type error under TypeScript's\n`strict` settings, and an `as string` there would be a second definition of the wire\ntype. The helper throws at start, where a missing variable is a deployment mistake you can see.\n\n**`instanceof Refusal`, never a cast.** A `catch` binds `unknown`, and the honest reason to\nnarrow rather than assert is that this `catch` also sees the errors you did not plan for. An\n`as Refusal` here would read a `clause` off a `TypeError` and report a protocol refusal that\nnever happened.\n\n**`canon` here, `json.dumps` in the Python example, and both are correct.** `verifyReceipt` parses\nyour bytes and canonicalises them itself, so any JSON spelling of the same object gives the same\nhash. `canon` is used here only because `@ziffer-io/verify` exports it; `new TextEncoder().encode(\nJSON.stringify(proposal))` verifies identically. Bytes that are not UTF-8 JSON at all are refused\nas `NotCanonicallyEncodable` (`AT-8a`), which is a bug in your call rather than a verdict on the receipt.\n\n**For an action that needed approval, the anchor needs `quorum`.** It is a `QuorumPolicy`\n(exported as a type by `@ziffer-io/verify`):\n`quorumK`, `attesters` (a map from identity to its credential), `assurances`, `minAssurance`,\n`policyBundleHash` and `bundleEpoch`, every one from your own signed bundle. Without it, a receipt\nthat carries approvals is refused `ApprovalsUnverifiable` (`AB-1`). `loadAttesterRegistry` reads the first four out of\n`attesters/registry.json` in your policy repository, and refuses a registry that is malformed,\nnames one key holder twice, or holds a weak key, by the same names the Python SDK uses. The last\ntwo name the policy version the approvals were given under, and the registry file does not carry\nthem: `policyBundleHash` is the tree hash `ziffer list` prints for that bundle and `bundleEpoch`\nis its `bundle_epoch`. `waitForReceipt` keeps waiting while the action is held, so give it\nminutes rather than seconds:\n\n```ts\nimport { loadAttesterRegistry } from '@ziffer-io/client';\n\nconst registry = await loadAttesterRegistry(env('ZIFFER_ATTESTER_REGISTRY')); // your attesters/registry.json\nconst heldAnchor: TrustAnchor = {\n  ...anchor,\n  quorum: { ...registry, policyBundleHash: POLICY_BUNDLE_HASH, bundleEpoch: BUNDLE_EPOCH }, // from `ziffer list`\n};\n\nconst decision = await client.waitForReceipt(submitted.decision_id, { timeoutMs: 15 * 60_000 });\nif (decision.receipt === undefined) {\n  throw new Error(`ziffer refused: ${decision.refusal_category}`);\n}\nconst result = verifyReceipt(decision.receipt, canon(proposal), heldAnchor);\nconsole.log('approved by', result.quorum?.approvals.join(', '));\n```\n\nThe module `ziffer-scan --code` writes takes those two values from the receipt's own signed\nbody instead, as the Python SDK does. That proves the approvals and the receipt name one policy\nversion; taking them from `ziffer list`, as above, also proves it is the version you published.\n\n**Checking an audit chain download**, under the `anchor-identity.pub` we handed you at enrolment\n(section 6 says what it checks, and why you compare its fingerprint first):\n\n```ts\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { parseAnchorKey, verifyChain } from '@ziffer-io/verify';\n\nconst key = parseAnchorKey(readFileSync('anchor-identity.pub'));\nconst result = verifyChain(readFileSync('ziffer-audit-example.json'), key); // throws ChainRefusal\nconsole.log(result.lastChainHash, result.anchoredThrough, result.unanchoredFrom);\n```\n\n<!-- guide:/typescript -->\n\n---\n\n## 10. The MCP-assisted path\n\nIf you are writing this integration with an AI assistant, you can give it direct access to\nthis guide and to a live decision loop:\n\n```bash\nnpx @ziffer-io/mcp\n```\n\nThat starts a local MCP server over stdio. Its tools fall into the four groups below, and there\nare no others.\n\nAbout your integration: `get_integration_guide` (this document, by language), `propose`\nand `check_decision` (the live loop, against whatever `ZIFFER_API_URL` you configure),\n`explain_receipt` (hand it a receipt and the proposal bytes and it tells you `valid` or the named\nclause), and `sandbox_status` (whether the tenant id you are sending is a sandbox, and whether one\ndecision is still waiting on its robot approver).\n\nAbout your key: `whoami` (which tenant your API key is bound to, and until when the key is\naccepted -- two facts and no more, because nothing an SDK call does needs your policy).\n\nAbout your policy repository and the rules in it: `get_policy_repo_guide` (its README and\nthe first-hour guide, verbatim), `check_policy_repo` (reads a clone and reports what is left to do\nin it), `explain_publish_failure` (reads a failed publish run's log and names the step, the\nrefusal and the fix), `explain_policy` (reads a policy tree and says, per action, whether it runs\nalone, runs with somebody told, is held for a quorum, or is refused and by what clause), and\n`simulate_decision` (grades one proposal against a draft tree and gives you the verdict and the\nclause).\n\n`explain_policy` and `simulate_decision` run `ziffer decide --unsigned` -- the same command your own pull request workflow runs\n-- rather than reading your rules a second time in TypeScript, so a verdict you read there is the\nverdict the engine grades. With `--unsigned` no signature is checked and no activation happens, so\nthey answer what your *rules* say and not whether a bundle would load.\n\nAbout getting started and reading answers: `get_started` (the steps in order, from\nnothing), `scan` and `explain_scan_finding` (every tool your code or your machine gives a model,\ngraded by the engine under a draft policy, and why each was graded that way), `check_integration`\n(each `propose` call site with no `verify` beside it), `lint_proposal` (one proposal against the\nwire schema, on your machine), `get_decision` (one decision, its receipt verified under your\ntrust anchor), `list_decisions` (section 6's list), `search_docs` and `explain_refusal` (these\nguides and the support guide's refusal table), and `send_feedback`, which sends the text you give\nit to us, to be read by a person.\n\nIt is configured by the same environment variables as the SDK, and **it starts without them.**\nTools that need a value return a named error saying which variable to set, rather than the\nserver dying during startup where an AI agent cannot see why. `get_integration_guide` needs nothing\nat all, so an AI agent can read this document before any credential exists.\n\nTwo things it deliberately does not have. There is **no approve tool and no simulated approval**:\nan approval minted on a developer's laptop is a fake receipt factory, which is precisely what this\nproduct exists to prevent. `sandbox_status` is not one: a ZIFFER sandbox is a separate tenant\napproved by a service inside our deployment, under keys your machine does not hold, so that tool\nreports and approves nothing. Neither is `simulate_decision`: it runs the grader, which answers\nwhat your rules say and signs nothing, and a `PASSED` from it is a grade rather than permission to\nact. And **no tool writes into your project**: nothing in that server creates, edits or deletes\na file in your repository, in your policy tree, or anywhere else you keep work. Two tools write\noutside it. `simulate_decision` writes the proposal into a temporary directory and removes it\nagain, because `--proposal` takes a path. `scan` writes its results into a fresh directory under\nthe system temporary directory and leaves them there for you to read: the draft policy folder\n`ziffer-policy-<date>` and, beside it, `ziffer-tools.json` (each tool's key and resource, which\nthe ZIFFER snippet reads), `ziffer-scan.json` (the result), and, unless you pass `report: false`,\nthe report `ziffer-scan-report.html` and the review archive `ziffer-review.tar.gz`. The one case in\nwhich those files land somewhere you choose is a confirmed scan of the installed servers with\n`out` naming a folder: they go there, and a folder inside your project is then yours to have\nnamed. `scan` starts the MCP servers your AI agent clients are configured to start only when\ncalled a second time with `confirm: true`. The server serves knowledge and ZIFFER-side calls;\nyour AI assistant edits your code.\n\nTo say it once more, because it is the sentence most easily lost: **MCP alone routes the\nquestion; only the SDK's `verify` line enforces the answer.** An integration written with the\nMCP server's help is gated by exactly the lines of `verify` that ended up in your handler.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Checklist\n\n- [ ] Your API key is in `ZIFFER_API_KEY` and is not in your source tree.\n- [ ] You know the date your key expires, and it is written down somewhere that will remind you.\n      The SDK warns for the last fourteen days, once per process and only while calls still\n      succeed, and an expired key then answers exactly like a key that never existed.\n- [ ] Your rotation is overlap-then-revoke: new key deployed and serving traffic BEFORE the old\n      one is revoked.\n- [ ] No request body sets a `tenant_id` other than your key's. The key is the tenant.\n- [ ] `ZIFFER_TRUST_ANCHOR` points at a `ziffer pubkey` document you obtained out of band,\n      not at a signing key and not at something the API served you.\n- [ ] `ZIFFER_SUITE_FLOOR` is set to a floor you chose.\n- [ ] If you check audit chain downloads, you pass the `anchor-identity.pub` we handed you, never\n      `anchor.json`, and you compared its fingerprint with the one we read to you on a second\n      channel first. Section 6.\n- [ ] Your handler calls `verify` **and branches on it**. Grep for the call; then grep for the\n      `if`. Section 7, item 1.\n- [ ] Your execution is idempotent on your own key. Section 7, item 2.\n- [ ] You keep the receipt somewhere you can produce it later.\n- [ ] You have read section 7 and know the six things that stay your code's job.\n- [ ] If you were given sandbox credentials, your production configuration still points at your\n      production key and anchor. Section 7, item 6.\n- [ ] You know where to write when something is refused, and what to put in the report.\n      The support guide has the addresses, the severities, and, in its section 4, a row per\n      refusal saying what it means and who moves next.\n";
